#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# take_largest

#% text
# `take_largest(xs, n)` gives the `n` largest elements of `xs`, largest first.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Elements are compared in the canonical order, so numbers by value.

#% item
# `take_smallest` gives the smallest ones.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
take_largest([3, 1, 4, 1, 5], 2)
#% output
#= [5, 4]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The values of an association, keeping their keys:

#% cell
take_largest(assoc(a -> 3, b -> 1, c -> 2), 2)
#% output
#= assoc(a -> 3, c -> 2)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The first elements of `reverse_sort`:

#% cell
xs = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5]
take_largest(xs, 3) == take(reverse_sort(xs), 3)
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `take_smallest`, `reverse_sort`, `max`, `max_by`
